The mask capture: content isolated on transparency for shader shadows.

A screen-shader region only ever sees the composed frame, where glyphs
and backdrop are already one image, so no shader could cast a shadow
from a panel's content alone. This adds the missing input. A caller
brackets a stretch of painting as a mask span (marker entries in the
frame's paint operations, so cached subtrees replay theirs), and when a
region whose chain binds `mask` draws, the renderer replays that span
into a window-sized layer over a transparent clear, using the main
pass's own pipelines, then crops the region's rect out beside the
`screen` scratch. The binding joins the template after `prev`, is
classified by the same drop-and-revalidate trick, and forces the region
pass the way `screen` does. Box shadows, video surfaces, in-scene user
shader quads and nested regions don't replay: their pixels stay on
screen, they just cast nothing.

Blade only for now. The DirectX renderer binds the register as an empty
SRV, which samples zeros, so a mask shader there runs against a
transparent mask instead of breaking.

The z- prefix is ordering, not taxonomy: patches apply in C-collation
glob order, this one diffs against nearly the whole user-shader series
plus windows-server-frames-opaque, so it has to sort last.

